.TH EM 1.SH NAMEem, me, umacs \- MicroEMACS screen editors.SH SYNOPSIS\fBem\fR[\-ve] [\-s\fIstring\fR] [\-g\fIline\fR] [\-r] [\-k\fIkey\fR] [@\fIfile\fR] [file ...].br\fBme\fR[\-cerv] [\-f\fIfile\fR] [\-g\fIline\fR] [\-s\fIstring\fR] [file ...].br\fBumacs\fR[file ...].SH DESCRIPTIONThree MicroEMACS editors: screen editors with Emacs keys..TP 12\fBem\fRMicroEMACS 3.8b..TP 12\fBme\fRMicroEMACS 3.11, with its messages and help in German\fR(\fBDatei\fRfor file)..TP 12\fBumacs\fRuMacs 1.0, a MicroEMACS cut down to 45K, with the same keys and no macrolanguage..PPThe keys common to all three: control-F, -B, -N and -P move forward,back, down and up; control-A and -E go to the start and end of the line;control-V and ESC V page down and up; ESC < and ESC > go to the startand end of the file; control-D deletes a character and control-K therest of the line; control-Y puts back what was killed; control-Ssearches.  control-X control-S saves, control-X control-F finds anotherfile, and control-X control-C leaves..PPEach needs\fBTERM\fRset to a terminal in the termcap:\fBem\fRstops with\fBEnvironment variable TERM not defined!\fRwithout it..SH OPTIONSFor\fBem\fR:\fR\fB\-v\fRview only,\fB\-e\fRedit,\fB\-s\fIstring\fRsearch for\fIstring\fRat the start,\fB\-g\fIline\fRgo to\fIline\fR,\fR\fB\-r\fRrestricted use,\fB\-k\fIkey\fRencrypt with\fIkey\fR,\fR\fB@\fIfile\fRa startup file other than\fB.emacsrc\fRin your home directory..PPFor\fBme\fR,\fRthe same letters in German help, and\fB\-f\fIfile\fRruns\fIfile\fRinstead of\fBemacs.rc\fRat the start,\fB\-c\fRmakes buffers changeable,\fB\-i\fIvar value\fRsets a variable..SH FILES.TP 12\fB/dd/SYS/umacs.hlp\fRuMacs's help..SH NOTES\fBumacs\fRneeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR\fBDOC/README-EDITORS\fRcompares these with the other editors here;\fBemacs\fRis MicroEMACS 4.00, the newest..SH SEE ALSOemacs, mg